// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// DA14585 - load an image into SysRAM and run it, via J-Link Commander.
//
//   JLinkExe -device Cortex-M0 -if SWD -speed 4000 -autoconnect 1 \
//            -CommanderScript ram_load.jlink
//
// Build-independent: no hardcoded MSP/PC. See "the second reset" below.
//
// Cortex-M0 has no VTOR, so the vector table must sit at address 0 -> the
// remap of address 0 to SysRAM is mandatory, not cosmetic. Without it every
// interrupt vectors into ROM, and the BLE stack is interrupt-driven.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

r
h

// Freeze the watchdog early: if the image faults we want the core to stay
// halted for inspection, not be reset back into the stock SPI-flash firmware
// (which destroys the evidence and looks like "our image never ran").
w2 0x50003300, 0x0008

// >>> EDIT THIS PATH <<< (J-Link Commander scripts do not expand env vars or
// ~, so it has to be a literal absolute path to your build output.)
loadfile /path/to/DA145xx_SDK/6.0.22.1401/projects/target_apps/template/hema_epd_clock/e2studio/DA14585/hema_epd_clock.elf

// --- the second reset -------------------------------------------------------
// 'loadfile' resets BEFORE it downloads, so the core latched MSP/PC from
// whatever image was in RAM at the time (usually the stock firmware). Running
// from those stale values jumps into the middle of a different program.
//
// Resetting again AFTER the download is meant to make the core re-latch MSP/PC
// from the freshly-written vector table, which is why this script has no
// hardcoded entry values (they shift on every build).
//
// *** THIS DOES NOT RELIABLY WORK ON AN OTP-BOOTING TAG. READ ON. ***
//
// This section used to claim the reset was safe because SYS_CTRL_REG's
// address-0 remap lives in the always-on domain and survives SYSRESETREQ, so
// the boot ROM never re-runs. Observed on a Type 4 on 2026-08-12, that is not
// what happens: after this reset the core came up with PC at the *flashed*
// image's reset handler rather than the loaded one, and moments later SysRAM no
// longer held the loaded code at all - compared byte for byte against the ELF.
// The boot chain had put the flashed image back.
//
// The failure is silent and expensive. The tag boots, BLE advertises, the panel
// works, symbols resolve, and every value you read is the OLD firmware
// answering questions about a build it is not. It cost most of a session, and
// what exposed it was not a crash but arithmetic: the numbers were stale rather
// than wrong-looking.
//
// So VERIFY, every time, before you believe anything you read:
//
//   1. mem32 0x07FC0000, 2 must show your build's MSP and reset handler
//        llvm-nm <your.elf> | grep ' T Reset_Handler'    (+1 for Thumb)
//   2. and then check actual CODE, not just the vector table - the table can
//      still hold your values while the text underneath has been replaced:
//        mem8 <addr of some function of yours>, 16
//      against
//        llvm-objdump -d --disassemble-symbols=<fn> <your.elf>
//
// If they disagree, the load did not take. Flash the image with tools/flash.sh
// instead; that path is reliable and is what the measurements in
// hema-local/docs/TAG_VARIANTS.md were taken through.
r
h

// Re-assert after that reset: freeze watchdog + remap (SDK's own init values).
w2 0x50003300, 0x0008
w2 0x50003102, 0x0001
w2 0x50003100, 0x0005
w2 0x50000012, 0x00A2

// Sanity check: word0 = initial MSP, word1 = reset handler (bit0 = Thumb).
// These should match the image you just built, not the stock firmware.
mem32 0x07FC0000, 2

g

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Diagnostics, if the panel does not update:
//
//   mem16 0x50003040, 1   P2_DATA - bit0 = BUSY. It should read high (000B
//                         with CS+PWR set) for ~1.6 s during a refresh, then
//                         drop back to 000A. Poll it every ~150 ms while the
//                         firmware boots. No BUSY pulse at all = the panel is
//                         not being driven.
//
//   Variant B pin modes, measured on a Type 4 running the bit-bang build.
//   Both variants bit-bang as of 2026-08-12, so P0_0 and P0_6 are plain GPIOs
//   between flash accesses - they read 0307/0306 only while the boot flash has
//   the bus, and catching them there means you sampled mid-transaction rather
//   than that the panel is on the SPI block.
//
//   mem16 0x50003006, 1   P0_0 mode, expect 0300 (output GPIO = SCK)
//   mem16 0x50003010, 1   P0_5 mode, expect 0300 (output GPIO = DC)
//   mem16 0x50003012, 1   P0_6 mode, expect 0300 (output GPIO = SDA)
//   mem16 0x50003014, 1   P0_7 mode, expect 0300 (output GPIO = RST)
//   mem16 0x50003046, 1   P2_0 mode, expect 0200 (input PULLDOWN = BUSY; the
//                         pull-down matters - see epd_gpio_init())
//   mem16 0x50003048, 1   P2_1 mode, expect 0300 (output GPIO = CS)
//   mem16 0x5000304C, 1   P2_3 mode, expect 0300 (output GPIO = PWR)
//   mem16 0x50001200, 1   SPI_CTRL, expect 1009 (the boot flash's config at
//                         4 MHz - the panel no longer configures this block;
//                         1011 is the old SPI-panel build, tools/build.sh
//                         --spi-panel)
//
// If the core sits at a fixed PC inside GPIO_ConfigurePin, that is the SDK's
// pin-allocation monitor hitting __BKPT(0) - a pin was configured without
// being declared in GPIO_reservations() (user_periph_setup.c).
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
